rg/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$3f:0:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n4b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"The amount of CPU, memory and local disk needed to run any database changes constantly over time.\\nFor example, here is the compute utilization of a typical database over a 24-hour period:\"}]\n4c:[\"$\",\"$La8\",null,{\"title\":\"Compute Usage of a Database over 24hrs\",\"datasetKey\":\"actual_compute_1d\",\"autoscalingOnly\":true,\"showStats\":false,\"compact\":true}]\n4d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"In the chart, a CU is an index of CPU, memory, and local file cache (LFC) utilization.\\n1 CU ≈ 1 CPU, 4GB RAM.\"}]\n4e:[\"$\",\"blockquote\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xl\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"How much compute should you buy to run this database?\"}]}]}]\n4f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"In an autoscaling platform compute is allocated automatically, while in a provisioned (non-autoscaling) platform the user must decide how much compute to buy.\"}]\n50:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"A provisioned platform is one where databases run on instances with a fixed amount of CPU, memory, and sometimes even disk.\\nTo help provisioned database users make informed compute size decisions, AWS has an \",[\"$\",\"$La9\",null,{\"href\":\"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/aws-cloud-financial-management/new-rightsizing-recommendations-for-amazon-rds-mysql-and-rds-postgresql-in-aws-compute-optimizer/\",\"children\":\"RDS Rightsizing\"}],\" tool that works by finding the P99.5 CPU and memory utilization over a lookback window and adding 20%.\"]}]\n51:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Here's what P99.5 + 20% looks like for our example database:\"}]\n52:[\"$\",\"$La8\",null,{\"title\":\"Provisioned: P99.5 + 20% (AWS Recommended)\",\"datasetKey\":\"actual_compute_1d\",\"showStats\":false,\"compact\":true,\"showOverprovisionSelector\":false}]\n53:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"You can think of \",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"p-1\",\"style\":{\"background-color\":\"rgb(255 166 76 / 0.2)\",\"color\":\"rgb(255 166 76)\"},\"children\":\"orange as wasted compute.\"}],\"\\nIt's cloud compute that we paid for, but it delivered zero value.\\nIt just sat there idle.\"]}]\n54:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Even when we over-provision, we see two \",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"bg-secondary-1/20 text-secondary-1 p-1\",\"children\":\"performance degradations in red\"}],\". This is because AWS rightsizing algorithm over-provisions 20% above \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"the P99.5%\"}],\" value of resource utilization. So the most extreme 0.5% resource peaks may still exhaust the available resources.\"]}]\n55:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"To save money, we could also under-provision \",[\"$\",\"em\",null,{\"children\":\"(i.e.: buy a smaller instance)\"}],\":\"]}]\n56:[\"$\",\"$La8\",null,{\"title\":\"Provisioned: Under-provision \",\"datasetKey\":\"actual_compute_1d\",\"showStats\":false,\"compact\":true,\"overprovision\":-20,\"showOverprovisionSelector\":false}]\n57:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"But now we see even more \",[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"bg-secondary-1/20 text-secondary-1 p-1\",\"children\":\"incidents in red\"}],\" where the database needs more compute than what is available.\\nAt these points we might experience degraded performance, even total failure.\"]}]\n58:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Autoscaling removes the sizing decision from the customer.\\nIt uses an algorithm to buy and allocate the right amount of compute at each point in time to optimally run the data"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"base workload.\"}]\n59:[\"$\",\"$La8\",null,{\"title\":\"Autoscaling: Compute allocated automatically to match workload\",\"datasetKeys\":[\"actual_compute_